  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nLight rain and sprinkles continue to affect western portions of\ncentral Wisconsin early this morning as a mid level shortwave\ntracks through the region. Rainfall amounts from this rain are\nvery light, on the order of a few hundredths of an inch as it\nfalls from a mid level deck given the abundant low level dry air\nin place. Although the shortwave is expected to track east through\nthe morning, dry air will eat away at the precipitation as it\nheads east. \n\nSome fog is possible behind the showers across central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin later this morning, which could affect the \nmorning commute, as winds decouple and remnant moisture gets \ntrapped under near-surface inversion. Surface observations show\nthis is already starting to come to fruition across far northwestern\nVilas County. Will issue SPS statements as appropriate as the \nscope and impact becomes clear.\n\nOtherwise, models continue to be unenthusiastic about rain \nchances over the weekend as Hudson Bay high dominates the synoptic\npattern. As such, PoPs are held down to around 10 to 20% mainly \nover central and north- central Wisconsin where models show the \ngreatest chance for showers. Strung out surface low then ejects \noff into northern Canada early Friday morning, resulting in \nshowers splitting to our north and south.\n\nNext chances for widespread stratiform rain remain on track for\nthe middle of next week as low pressure organizes over the\ncentral Plains and deepening trough passes over the region.\nThough ensembles still diverge on exact timing of precip,\nconsensus shows PWATs reaching near 90th percentile relative to\nclimo, increasing confidence in at least a soaking rainfall.\nProbabilistic guidance currently shows a 30 to 50% signal for \nreceiving one inch of rainfall, though trends will need to \ncontinue to be monitored. Otherwise, seasonal temperatures in the\n60s to low 70s prevail for the extended.",
